---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-sarcoidosis`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Sarcoidosis',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Sarcoidosis', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'D86', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Sarcoidosis', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Sarcoidosis: Why Supplements Can Be Dangerous Here"
  description="Sarcoidosis is the classic condition where vitamin D flips from helpful to harmful. Granulomas make active vitamin D uncontrollably, risking hypercalcaemia. What patients need to know."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-sarcoidosis", label: "Vitamin D & Sarcoidosis" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Sarcoidosis</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Almost everywhere on this site, vitamin D is something to top up. Sarcoidosis is the important
      exception. It is the textbook example of a disease in which ordinary — even modest — vitamin D
      supplementation can cause a dangerously high blood calcium. If you have sarcoidosis, the rules
      you have read elsewhere do not apply to you without medical supervision.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Why the normal safety valve fails</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      In healthy people, the kidney tightly controls how much 25(OH)D is converted to the active
      hormone 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol): when calcium is adequate, conversion is switched
      off. In sarcoidosis, the granulomas themselves — clusters of activated immune cells — express
      the activating enzyme <em>1α-hydroxylase</em>, and this production is <strong>unregulated</strong>.
      Feed the pathway more raw material (25(OH)D, whether from sun or supplements) and the granulomas
      keep making active hormone regardless of calcium status. The result can be hypercalcaemia
      (roughly 10% of patients) and hypercalciuria (up to a third), which threaten the kidneys.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The paradox of low 25(OH)D</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Confusingly, many sarcoidosis patients have <em>low</em> 25(OH)D on testing — precisely because
      it is being rapidly converted to the active form. A low 25(OH)D here should not be reflexively
      "corrected" the way it would be in someone without the disease. What matters is the
      <strong>active</strong> 1,25(OH)₂D and the serum and urine calcium, which is why specialists
      often measure the active hormone rather than acting on 25(OH)D alone. Sun exposure can raise
      calcium too, so some patients are advised to moderate it and to be cautious with high-dose
      fortified products.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What patients should do</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Do not start vitamin D on your own.</strong> Any supplementation should be decided and monitored by the team managing your sarcoidosis.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch calcium, not just 25(OH)D.</strong> Serum and urinary calcium guide safety; a "low" 25(OH)D can be misleading.</li>
      <li><strong>Bone health still matters.</strong> Corticosteroids used to treat sarcoidosis themselves weaken bone, so bone protection is handled carefully and individually — sometimes with cautious, monitored vitamin D and calcium.</li>
      <li><strong>Report symptoms of high calcium</strong> — excessive thirst and urination, nausea, constipation, confusion, kidney stones — promptly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The wider lesson</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Sarcoidosis is the best-known granulomatous cause of vitamin D-mediated hypercalcaemia, but the
      same mechanism operates in tuberculosis, some lymphomas, and other granulomatous diseases. It is
      a reminder that "vitamin D is safe and everyone should take more" is a population statement with
      real exceptions. For most people the
      <a href="/vitamin-d-toxicity" class="text-amber-700 underline">toxicity threshold</a> is very
      high; in these conditions it is not.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice — and an important safety note.</strong> If you have sarcoidosis or
      another granulomatous disease, do not take vitamin D supplements without your specialist's
      guidance and calcium monitoring. This page is educational and does not replace that care.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-sarcoidosis" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
